##### 8. Exception Handling in Tests — `addNullSkuManualCatch()`

**Lines 93–98.** Manual `try/catch` + `fail()` instead of JUnit 5's `assertThrows`. The `catch` only checks `assertNotNull(ex.getMessage())` — it doesn't verify the exception type, so any exception passes.

```java
try {
    catalog.add(null, 1);
    fail("should have thrown");
} catch (Exception ex) {
    assertNotNull(ex.getMessage());
}
```

**Fix:** Use `assertThrows`:

```java
@Test
void addNullSkuThrows() {
    IllegalArgumentException ex = assertThrows(
        IllegalArgumentException.class,
        () -> catalog.add(null, 1));
    assertTrue(ex.getMessage().contains("sku is required"));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Any exception type (even `NullPointerException`) satisfies the catch; the test may pass for the wrong reason. The `fail()` call can also be accidentally deleted.

